  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMain storm activity to remain north and northwest of ILN/s area\naround the periphery of the mid level ridge this evening. A frontal \nboundary encroaching into the Great Lakes will lead to increasing \nprecipitation chances overnight into Wednesday morning. Storms that \nare expected to develop to our northwest this evening are expected to\nmove into west central Ohio late this evening through the overnight \nhours. SPC Day 1 outlook has adjusted the marginal risk area a little\nfarther north. This looks very reasonable with some of the CAM \nsolutions showing scattered convection moving into ILN/s northern \ncounties. The storms are expected to be weakening and shear and \ninstability is not overly impressive, but can not rule out a marginal\nsevere threat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nHeat and humidity will be observed through Thursday with ILN/s area\non the northern periphery of the mid level ridge. Convective chances\nlook most favorable across our northern counties - from the frontal \nzone on the northern edge of the warm sector. Although convection \nand clouds could alter the temperature forecast, at this time \nWednesday and Thursday look to be the warmest days. With surface\ndewpoints getting into the lower 70s, some areas in the southern \nhalf of the forecast area may approach heat advisory criteria (100 \ndegree heat indices) on both days. \n\nLooking at forecast soundings - the environment will be characterized \nby marginal shear and moderate instability which suggests a lack of \nan organized severe weather setup but a strong to severe storm can \nnot be ruled out. Heavy rain looks to be a threat with PWAT values \naround 2 inches and the potential for several rounds of storms. \n\nThe ridge begins to break down on Friday and into the weekend, \noffering slightly cooler temperatures. Thunderstorm chances continue\nin the west- northwest flow pattern around the ridge centered over \nthe Lower MS Valley.",
